{0 Conventions: how the bindings are organised}

{1 Who this page is for}

This page explains how the bindings are organised and how GTK's API
maps onto OCaml. 

It is for anyone working with the bindings who wishes to get started
quickly by learning the bindings' semantics, as well as for more
advanced users who need to understand how they interact with the GObject/GLib
C layer.

It assumes you know basic OCaml, including how to use the class language (classes
and objects) and polymorphic variants.

{1 Background on GObject}

GObject (or the GLib Object system) defines a portable object model 
for C-based code in a way that makes it straightforward to bind 
and use from other languages. GObject is the cornerstone of GTK
and its dependencies, as well as libraries that depend on it like
GNOME's Adwaita.

At the core of GObject is a generic, introspectable type system
called GType. This runtime description facilitates cross-language
bindings, which can be done either:

- dynamically at runtime (e.g.
in loosely typed languages like JavaScript or Python) 
- statically at compile time, where the metadata is used to procedurally generate binding code for strongly typed languages via {b GObject Introspection} 
(like Rust or OCaml) 

({i We've used the latter approach}).

The GType system can handle primitives like strings, numbers, booleans,
etc., as well as objects and classes (using a single-parent inheritance
structure but allowing multiple interfaces, similar to Java or Objective-C).
It contains enough metadata to specify how reference counting should
operate (for objects) and how ownership is transferred (for both objects and
primitives) so that memory can be managed from the host
language runtime without any special conventions required by the developer.

GObject also defines {b closures}, which allow the C-language code to
call back into the host language; and {b signals}, which are events
emitted on an object, that can be listened to via a closure. (You will
interact with signals by registering an OCaml callback function - 
closures are an internal detail).

{1:libraries Libraries and namespaces}

Each GObject namespace (apart from GLib and GObject, which share [ocglib])
is its own opam package, containing one library of
the same name. The name is [oc] followed by the namespace name in lower
case, and the library's top-level module is that name capitalised. Add the
library for each namespace you use to the [libraries] field of your dune
file, for example [(libraries ocgtk ocgio)].

{t | Namespace | opam package and dune library | Top-level module |
   | --------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------- |
   | Gtk       | [ocgtk]        | {!Ocgtk} |
   | Gdk       | [ocgdk]        | {!Ocgdk} |
   | Gsk       | [ocgsk]        | {!Ocgsk} |
   | Gio       | [ocgio]        | {!Ocgio} |
   | Pango     | [ocpango]      | {!Ocpango} |
   | PangoCairo | [ocpangocairo] | {!Ocpangocairo} |
   | cairo     | [occairo]      | {!Occairo} |
   | GdkPixbuf | [ocgdkpixbuf]  | {!Ocgdkpixbuf} |
   | Graphene  | [ocgraphene]   | {!Ocgraphene} |
   | GLib and GObject | [ocglib] | {!Ocglib} |}

Installing [ocgtk] installs all of these, because GTK depends on them.

Every generated library has the same layout:

{[
Oc<namespace>
  +- <Class>                 (* one module per class, interface or record (layer 2) *)
  +- Raw                     (* functional modules (layer 1) *)
  |    +- <Class>
  +- Enums                   (* enumerations and bitfields *)
  +- Constants               (* constants *)
]}

For example, GTK's library {!Ocgtk} looks like this:

{[
Ocgtk
  +- Window, Button, Application, ...
  +- Raw                     (* Raw.Window, Raw.Button, ... *)
  +- Enums
  +- Constants
  +- Main                    (* GTK only: initialisation and main loop *)
  +- Version                 (* GTK only: version information *)
]}

See {!Ocgtk.Raw}, {!Ocgtk.Enums}, {!Ocgtk.Constants}, {!Ocgtk.Main} and
{!Ocgtk.Version}.

{!Ocglib} is different: it is written by hand rather than generated, and has
no [Raw] layer. It holds the GLib and GObject modules ({!Ocglib.Glib},
{!Ocglib.Gobject}, {!Ocglib.GError} and others) and the fixed-width integer
types (see {{!section-integers}Integers}). The examples on this page assume
it is opened, so they write [Gobject] for {!Ocglib.Gobject} and [Glib] for
{!Ocglib.Glib}. Either add [open Ocglib] to your file or
[(flags (:standard -open Ocglib))] to your dune stanza. Short examples that
don't show their [open] lines also assume [open Ocgtk].

The libraries are designed so that you can open the top-level module and
then refer to each class module directly, e.g.:

{[
open Ocgtk

let activate app =
  let window = Window.new_ () in
  window#set_default_size 300 200;
  app#add_window window;
  window#present ()

let () =
  let app = Application.new_ (Some "org.example.Hello") [ `DEFAULT_FLAGS ] in
  let _ = app#on_activate ~callback:(fun () -> activate app) () in
  exit (app#run 0 None)
]}

See {{!section-mainloop}The main loop and threads} for what [Application] does
and the alternatives.

{1 The two layers}

OCaml is a functional language with an object system on top. GTK is
object-oriented, so OCaml classes are the natural way to use it. But OCaml
can only bind a C function as a value in a module (an [external]
declaration), not as a method of a class. So ocgtk is built in two layers:

- {b Layer 1: functions}. One module per GObject class, with an abstract
  handle type [t] and one function per C function, taking the handle as its
  first argument. This is the direct binding to C.
- {b Layer 2: classes and objects}. One OCaml class per GObject class / interface,
  whose methods call the Layer 1 functions. This is the layer most code
  should use.

{@mermaid[
flowchart TD
  C["C library (libgtk-4, libgio-2.0, ...)"]
  L1["Layer 1: Ocgtk.Raw.Button
  type t, one function per C function"]
  L2["Layer 2: Ocgtk.Button
  class type button_t, class button, new_* constructors"]
  APP["Your code"]
  C --> L1 --> L2
  APP -- "usually" --> L2
  APP -. "interop, low level" .-> L1
]}

(Internally, the C binding layer is known as "Layer 0").

You can use each layer independently of the other one, but because the classes
layer wraps around the functional layer, you can also move back and forth as
needed.

{2:anatomy Anatomy of a class module}

Known internally as "Layer 2", this is the recommended way of interacting
with ocgtk. Each GObject class and interface is mapped to an OCaml class which you
can invoke methods on. To create an object, use the constructor functions
described below rather than OCaml's [new]. ([new] is only needed when
turning a Layer 1 handle into an object; see
{{!section-converting}Converting between the layers}.)

Each class and its type is declared in a separate module. The module is named after the GObject class. It will also contain one or more constructor functions that instantiate an object of that class for you.

For example the [Window] class, class type and constructors are available in the {!Ocgtk.Window} module, which looks like this:

{@ocaml[
(* The class type, returned by a constructor function *)
class type window_t 

(* The class itself, never used directly (only if you are bridging L1 -> L2).
   It takes the Layer 1 handle, Raw.Window.t, which is
   [ `window | `widget | `initially_unowned | `object_ ] Gobject.obj *)
class window : Raw.Window.t -> window_t

(* Constructor, used to create a new instance *)
val new_ : unit -> window_t
]}

Class types are named in full so that they can be disambiguated in hover
and type-preview via LSP/merlin.

{@mermaid[
flowchart LR
  subgraph NS["Ocgtk (library top-level module)"]
    direction TB
    subgraph L2M["Button (Layer 2)"]
      CT["class type button_t
      methods, signals, properties"]
      CL["class button : Raw.Button.t -> button_t"]
      CTOR["new_ / new_with_label / ...
      return button_t"]
    end
    subgraph W["Raw"]
      L1M["Button (Layer 1)
      type t, new_, get_label, set_label, ..."]
    end
    ENUMS["Enumerations: polymorphic variants"]
    CONSTS["Constants"]
  end
  CTOR --> CL --> CT
  CL -. wraps .-> L1M
]}

{b Why one module per class?} The main reason is practicality: OCaml's
compiler won't easily handle a file with hundreds of class types and classes,
each with dozens of methods. Splitting them up also lets them compile in
parallel (which is much faster) and means unused classes aren't linked into
your application and don't bloat its binary size.

{2:converting Converting between the layers}

You can build entirely in Layer 1 or entirely in Layer 2, but you can also
mix and match as needed.

{3 Layer 2 -> Layer 1}

Instances of classes have [as_<class>] methods, and interfaces have [as_<interface>]
methods, which return the [t] handle for use in Layer 1.

For example:

{[
open Ocgtk

let () =
  let window = Window.new_ () in
  (* as_window gives us Raw.Window.t *)
  let window_handle = window#as_window in
  Raw.Window.unminimize window_handle;

  (* Window inherits from Widget, so as_widget gives us a Raw.Widget.t *)
  let widget_handle = window#as_widget in
  let direction = Raw.Widget.get_direction widget_handle in
  Printf.printf "Window text direction: %s\n" (match direction with
    | `NONE -> "none" | `LTR -> "left-to-right" | `RTL -> "right-to-left");
]}

NOTE: a Layer 1 handle can also be upcast directly to the handle type of any
of its parent classes or interfaces, without calling [as_*], e.g.

{[
  let widget_handle = (window_handle :> Raw.Widget.t) in
  ...
]}

{3 Layer 1 -> Layer 2}

You can promote a handle from Layer 1 to Layer 2 by simply instantiating
its class with the handle as the first argument to the constructor, e.g.:

{[
open Ocgtk

let () =
  let window_handle = Raw.Window.new_ () in
  let window = new Window.window window_handle in
  window#unminimize ();
]}

{3 Comparing objects}

Each time an object comes back from GTK (from [get_child], or as a signal
argument, for example), you get a new OCaml value wrapping it. Two Layer 2
objects can therefore refer to the same GTK object but compare unequal with
[==] or [=]. To check whether they are the same object, compare their Layer 1
handles:

{[
let same_widget (a : Widget.widget_t) (b : Widget.widget_t) =
  Gobject.same a#as_widget b#as_widget
]}

Layer 1 handles also support [=], [compare] and [Hashtbl.hash] by object
identity, so you can use them as keys in a [Hashtbl] or [Map].

{1 Using objects}

{2 Creating objects}

Use a constructor in a class's module to instantiate it, for example:
{[
open Ocgtk

let window = Window.new_ () in
let button = Button.new_with_label "Click me" in
...
]}

GTK must be initialised before you create any widget. [Application] does
this for you before it emits [activate], so create your widgets in (or after)
your [on_activate] handler. Without an [Application], call {!Ocgtk.Main.init} first
(see {{!section-mainloop}The main loop and threads}).

{b Interfaces} are also classes in OCaml, but they won't have constructor functions. The same applies to {b abstract classes}. For both of these, you will get values indirectly as the result (return values) of calling methods on other objects or as parameters in signal handlers.

{2 Inheritance and interfaces}

Classes and interfaces are represented as a class type and a class in OCaml. The OCaml class type for each GObject class/interface will inherit both its base class and each of its interfaces.

(Unlike GObject's single-inheritance + interfaces, OCaml supports multiple inheritance).

For example in GObject, the [Window] class inherits from [Widget] and implements [Accessible], [Buildable], [ConstraintTarget], [Native], [Root] and [ShortcutManager].

In OCaml, this will look something like:

{[
class type window_t = object
  (* base class *)
  inherit Widget.widget_t

  (* implements *)
  (* The other interfaces would appear here, but widget_t already inherits them, so they are skipped *)
  inherit Native.native_t
  inherit Root.root_t
  inherit ShortcutManager.shortcut_manager_t

end
]}

Note that [Widget] already implements [Accessible], [Buildable] and [ConstraintTarget], so they won't be added again to the definition of [Window].

{2:upcasting Upcasting: how to pass subclass values and use polymorphism}

Say you have a [button_t], but you want to pass it as a value to a method that
declares a parameter of [widget_t]. Unlike other object-oriented languages where you
can pass this directly, OCaml requires you to explicitly upcast it using the [:>]
operator.

For example, [Box#append] takes a [widget_t], so a button must be upcast before
it can be added to a box:

{[
open Ocgtk

let build_layout () =
  let box = Box.new_ `VERTICAL 10 in
  let button = Button.new_with_label "Click me" in

  (* Correct: upcast with :> *)
  let () = box#append (button :> Widget.widget_t) in
  box
]}

Without the upcast, [box#append button] is a type error, because [button] has
type [button_t] but [append] expects a [widget_t].

The same applies wherever a method takes a [widget_t]. For example,
[window#set_child (Some (box :> Widget.widget_t))] passes a [box_t option] to a
parameter of type [widget_t option].

{2:downcasting Downcasting: getting a subclass back}

Methods often return a parent type. For example, [window#get_child ()]
returns a [Widget.widget_t option], even if the child you added was a button.
OCaml's [:>] only goes up the hierarchy, and there is no built-in checked
downcast in ocgtk.

You can write one with the GObject type system, which knows the real class of
every object at runtime:

{[
open Ocgtk

let button_of_widget (w : Widget.widget_t) : Button.button_t option =
  let handle = w#as_widget in
  let button_type = Gobject.Type.from_name "GtkButton" in
  if Gobject.Type.is_a (Gobject.get_type handle) button_type then
    Some (new Button.button (Gobject.unsafe_cast handle))
  else None

let relabel (window : Window.window_t) =
  match window#get_child () with
  | Some child -> (
      match button_of_widget child with
      | Some button -> button#set_label "Found it"
      | None -> ())
  | None -> ()
]}

How it works:

- [w#as_widget] gets the Layer 1 handle (see
  {{!section-converting}Converting between the layers}).
- {!Ocglib.Gobject.get_type} returns the object's actual runtime type, and
  {!Ocglib.Gobject.Type.is_a} checks it against the type you want. It returns [true]
  for subclasses too (a [GtkToggleButton] is a [GtkButton]), and works for
  interfaces such as ["GtkRoot"].
- {!Ocglib.Gobject.Type.from_name} takes the {i C} type name: [GtkButton], not
  [Button] or [button_t].
- {!Ocglib.Gobject.unsafe_cast} changes the handle's type without any check, and
  [new Button.button] wraps it in a Layer 2 object.

Always do the [is_a] check first. Without it, [unsafe_cast] lets you call
button methods on an object that isn't a button, which will crash or corrupt
memory instead of raising an exception.

{2 Cyclic groups and top-level classes}

You may notice modules like [Application_and__window_and__window_group] (in {!Ocgtk}) or [App_launch_context_cycle_de440b34] (in {!Ocgdk}, where the joined name would be too long) in the module lists, and in the source of the classes that make them up. This is because some GObject classes and/or interfaces have mutually recursive dependencies between them.

In OCaml, these can't be declared in separate files, but instead need to be declared in an OCaml recursive cluster in the same file i.e. [class type c1 ... and c2 ... end] / [class c1 ... and c2 ... end]. Each individual class is then aliased in a separate module to "flatten" the namespace and hide the recursive classes.

{1:signals Signals}

In both Layer 1 and Layer 2, signals are exposed as a [on_<signal_name>] method/function. They take:

- an [?after] boolean argument, which defaults to [false]. If you set it to [true] it runs your handler after the other user-provided signal handlers that did not set the flag
- a labelled [~callback] argument (mandatory) that specifies your callback function

In Layer 2, the method also takes a final [()] argument (after the optional
[?after]), so that it is fully applied:

{[
let id = button#on_clicked ~callback:(fun () -> print_endline "clicked") () in
...
]}

NOTE: Forgetting the [()] leaves a partially applied function, so the handler is
never connected. The compiler warns about this (warning 5) if you pass the
result to [ignore], but not if you write [let _ = ...], so prefer [ignore] or
keep the handler ID.

The Layer 1 functions take the object as an argument and need no trailing
[()]:

{[
let id = Raw.Button.on_clicked handle ~callback:(fun () -> print_endline "clicked") in
...
]}

The return type is a {b signal handler ID} ({!Ocglib.Gobject.Signal.handler_id}). You
can ignore it (e.g. [let _ = b#on_clicked ~callback ()] or
[ignore (b#on_clicked ~callback ())]), but you should keep it if you will need
to disconnect the handler, for example when the object it updates goes away.

The functions for working with a handler ID live in {!Ocglib.Gobject.Signal}. They
take the Layer 1 handle, which you get from the [as_<class>] method (see
{{!section-converting}Converting between the layers}):

{[
Gobject.Signal.disconnect button#as_button id
]}

{!Ocglib.Gobject.Signal.handler_block}, {!Ocglib.Gobject.Signal.handler_unblock} and
{!Ocglib.Gobject.Signal.handler_is_connected}
take the same arguments.

The callback function's arguments are labelled e.g. [~x ~y ~width ~height].

{b Exceptions raised in a callback do not propagate.} GTK calls your callback
from an (indeterminate) point in the event loop, so an exception has nowhere 
to "unwind" back through to your code. ocgtk catches the exception, logs it 
as a GLib warning ([OCaml closure exception: Failure("...")]) and carries 
on as if the callback had returned normally. Your program keeps running, so 
watch stderr, or catch exceptions inside the callback if you need to handle them.

{@mermaid[
sequenceDiagram
  participant App as Your code
  participant B as button_t
  participant G as GObject signal system
  App->>B: b#35;on_clicked ~callback ()
  B->>G: connect "clicked"
  G-->>App: handler id
  Note over G: user clicks the button
  G->>App: callback ()
]}

{1 Properties}

Properties are mapped to [get_<property_name>] and [set_<property_name>] methods
in the class and function layer.

If a property specifies a specific accessor method, this is wrapped in preference
to generating a property accessor.

{1:mainloop The main loop and threads}

GTK is event driven. Your program builds its widgets, hands control to a
{b main loop}, and from then on your code only runs inside callbacks: signal
handlers, timeouts and idle functions.

There are three ways to run the loop. They all run the same GLib loop
underneath, so timeouts, idle callbacks and signals work the same with any
of them:

- {!Ocgtk.Application}: the standard way to write a GTK 4 application. Use
  this unless you have a reason not to.
- {!Ocgtk.Main}: a small module for running GTK without an application object.
  Useful for tests, examples and short scripts.
- {!Ocglib.Glib.Main}: the GLib main loop itself, for running a nested loop or
  creating your own loop objects.

{2 Running an application}

{!Ocgtk.Application} initialises GTK, runs the main loop, and quits when its
last window closes. Create your windows in the [activate] signal handler
and register them with [add_window] (or create an [Application_window]):

{[
open Ocgtk

let activate app =
  let window = Window.new_ () in
  window#set_title (Some "Hello");
  window#set_default_size 300 200;
  app#add_window window;
  window#present ()

let () =
  let app = Application.new_ (Some "org.example.Hello") [ `DEFAULT_FLAGS ] in
  let _ = app#on_activate ~callback:(fun () -> activate app) () in
  exit (app#run 0 None)
]}

- The application ID ([org.example.Hello]) is a reverse-DNS name that
  identifies your application to the desktop. With [`DEFAULT_FLAGS], a
  second launch activates the first instance instead of starting a new
  one.
- [app#run] blocks until the application quits, and returns an exit
  status for [exit].
- [app#run] takes C's [argc] and [argv] as separate arguments. [0 None]
  ignores the command line. To let GTK see it, pass
  [(Array.length Sys.argv) (Some Sys.argv)], and make sure the count matches
  the array's length.
  This is tracked in {{:https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk/issues/202}issue #202}.
- [app#quit ()] stops the application from anywhere. The [startup] and
  [shutdown] signals let you run code once, before the first [activate] and
  after the loop ends.

{2 Without an application: Main}

{!Ocgtk.Main} runs GTK without an application object:

{[
open Ocgtk

let () =
  let _ = Main.init () in
  let window = Window.new_ () in
  window#set_default_size 300 200;
  ignore (window#on_close_request ~callback:(fun () -> Main.quit (); false) ());
  window#present ();
  Main.main ()
]}

- {!Ocgtk.Main.init} initialises GTK. Call it before creating any widget. 
- {!Ocgtk.Main.main} runs the loop until {!Ocgtk.Main.quit} is called. Unlike an
  [Application], nothing quits automatically when the last window closes,
  so connect [close-request] as above.

{2 GLib main loops}

{!Ocgtk.Main.main} and {!Ocgtk.Main.quit} are shortcuts for a single {!Ocglib.Glib.Main.t}
loop that gives more control than {!Ocgtk.Main.main} on its own. Use 
{!Ocglib.Glib.Main} directly when you need a loop of your own,
for example to wait for something while keeping the UI responsive:

{[
let wait_until (ready : bool ref) =
  let loop = Glib.Main.create false in
  let _ =
    Glib.Timeout.add ~ms:100 ~callback:(fun () ->
      if !ready then (Glib.Main.quit loop; false) else true) ()
  in
  Glib.Main.run loop   (* returns once [ready] is set *)
]}

NOTE: {!Ocglib.Glib.Main} doesn't initialise GTK - you will still need to do
that manually with {!Ocgtk.Main.init} first.


{2 Timeouts and idle callbacks}

{!Ocglib.Glib.Timeout.add} runs a function every [ms] milliseconds, and
{!Ocglib.Glib.Idle.add} runs a function when the event loop is idle (i.e. no higher priority
tasks are waiting to run). 

The return value in your callback functions controls whether the timeout is
repeating (rescheduled on the event loop) or just run once. Return [true] from
your callback to have it repeat, or return [false] to remove it (don't schedule again).

Both also return an id you can pass to {!Ocglib.Glib.Timeout.remove} or {!Ocglib.Glib.Idle.remove}
(useful if you need to cancel the timeout/idle based on the result of another process).

{[
let () =
  let _ = Main.init () in
  let ticks = ref 0 in
  let _ =
    Glib.Timeout.add ~ms:50 ~callback:(fun () ->
      incr ticks;
      Printf.printf "tick %d\n%!" !ticks;
      if !ticks = 3 then (Main.quit (); false) else true) ()
  in
  let _ = Glib.Idle.add (fun () -> print_endline "idle"; false) in
  Main.main ()
(* idle / tick 1 / tick 2 / tick 3, then Main.main returns *)
]}

Callbacks run on the main loop, so a slow callback freezes the UI. Keep them
short, and split long jobs into smaller chunks, for example with an idle callback that
does a little work each time it runs.

{2 Threads}

GTK is not thread safe. Treat every ocgtk call as main-thread-only: create
and modify widgets, connect signals and so on from the thread that is
running the main loop. GLib's [g_idle_add] can be called from
any thread, which is why handing results back to the main thread through
{!Ocglib.Glib.Idle.add} is the recommended pattern (see below).

There is also an OCaml-specific caveat. None of the main loops ([app#run],
{!Ocgtk.Main.main} or {!Ocglib.Glib.Main.run}) release the OCaml runtime lock while they
wait for events. Other OCaml threads (from the [Thread] module) are
therefore {b starved} for as long as the loop is running. This is tracked
in {{:https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk/issues/203}issue #203}.
The workaround below works with all of them.

To let worker threads make progress, give them a chance to run by calling
[Thread.yield] from a timeout:

{[
let () =
  let _ = Main.init () in
  let _ = Glib.Timeout.add ~ms:20 ~callback:(fun () -> Thread.yield (); true) () in
  let _ =
    Thread.create (fun () ->
      let result = (* long computation or blocking I/O *) 42 in
      (* hand the result back to the main thread *)
      ignore (Glib.Idle.add (fun () ->
        Printf.printf "result: %d\n%!" result;  (* safe to touch widgets here *)
        Main.quit (); false)))
      ()
  in
  Main.main ()
]}

The program needs [threads.posix] in its dune [libraries] to use [Thread].

{2 Pumping the event loop}

If you would rather own the loop, you can pump it yourself and let other
threads run between iterations:

{[
while not !finished do
  ignore (Main.iteration false);
  Thread.delay 0.01
done
]}

(The delay, or a yield, is what lets other threads run without the lock
being held. Without doing this, iterating in a tight loop will starve 
the other threads' OCaml code by giving them no opportunity to run).

{1 Memory management}

For the most part, ocgtk manages GObject memory for you by ensuring that
GObject memory referenced by OCaml code is cleaned up when your OCaml values
are freed by the garbage collector. 

There is no GObject [unref], [free] or
[destroy]-to-free call to make; the C functions for these are deliberately
left unbound. An object is freed once GTK no longer needs it {i and} no
OCaml value refers to it, at which point the OCaml garbage collector
releases it. Constructors take full ownership of the new object, so there
is nothing extra to do when you add a widget to a container or remove it.

There are two exceptions that you need to be aware of:

- Closing a window or removing a widget doesn't free it immediately while
  you still hold it in an OCaml value. That is normally harmless. Calling
  methods on a closed window is safe, but has no visible effect.
- The OCaml garbage collector only sees the small OCaml value, not the
  memory the C object owns. If you create many large non-OCaml objects in a loop
  (images, textures, big text buffers) and drop them, memory can grow until
  the next major collection. Call [Gc.full_major ()] after such a burst if
  this matters.

{2 Signal handlers that capture their object}

A connected signal handler stays alive until it is disconnected, and so
does everything its closure refers to. Destroying a window or removing a
widget does {b not} disconnect handlers. So if a handler refers to the
object it is connected to, directly or through another object that refers
to it (a parent window, say), that object can never be freed until you
disconnect it.

Whether this matters depends on how long the objects live:

- {b Objects that live as long as the program}, such as the main window,
  its widgets and the [Application]. They are never freed anyway, so you
  don't need to keep handler IDs or disconnect anything.
- {b Objects you create repeatedly and throw away}, such as dialogs,
  popovers, widgets built for each item in a list, or a model you replace.
  Each one that has a handler referring back to itself leaks. Keep the
  handler IDs and disconnect them when you are done with the object.

For example, a dialog created each time the user asks for it, where the
OK button's handler refers to the dialog (which in turn holds the button).
Both handlers are disconnected when the dialog closes, so the dialog and
its button can be freed:

{[
let show_dialog (parent : Window.window_t) =
  let dialog = Window.new_ () in
  dialog#set_transient_for (Some parent);
  let ok = Button.new_with_label "OK" in
  dialog#set_child (Some (ok :> Widget.widget_t));
  let ok_id = ok#on_clicked ~callback:(fun () -> dialog#close ()) () in
  let close_id = ref None in
  close_id :=
    Some
      (dialog#on_close_request
         ~callback:(fun () ->
           Gobject.Signal.disconnect ok#as_button ok_id;
           Option.iter (Gobject.Signal.disconnect dialog#as_window) !close_id;
           false (* let the window close *))
         ());
  dialog#present ()
]}

(The [close-request] handler refers to [dialog] too, so it has to
disconnect itself. A handler may disconnect itself while it is running.)

{2:memory_records Records}

Each record value in OCaml owns its C memory, which is freed by the
garbage collector, so there is nothing to free by hand. See
{{!section-records}Record Types}.

{1:reference Reference: how GObject maps to OCaml}

This section is for looking things up: how each kind of GObject
name and type appears in OCaml.

{2:classes Classes, methods, signals and properties}

The following table shows how various things are mapped between GObject (in the GIR files and GObject documentation) and the equivalent:
- C declarations 
- OCaml for classes (Layer 2)  
- OCaml modules/functions (Layer 1).

{t | Component | GObject | C | Class Layer (L2) | Module Layer (L1) |
   | --------- | ------- | --- | ---------------- | ----------------- |
   | Methods   | set_label | [gtk_button_set_label(b, "x")] | [b#set_label "x"] | [Raw.Button.set_label b "x"] |
   | Instance Type | Button | [GtkButton] | [Ocgtk.Button.button_t] | [Ocgtk.Raw.Button.t] |
   | Constructors | new_with_label | [gtk_button_new_with_label("x")] | [Ocgtk.Button.new_with_label "x"] | [Ocgtk.Raw.Button.new_with_label "x"] |
   | Signals | clicked | [g_signal_connect(button, "clicked", ...)] | [b#on_clicked ~callback:(fun () -> ...) ()] |  [Raw.Button.on_clicked b ~callback:(fun () -> ...)] |
   | Properties (setters) | icon-name | [g_value_set_string(&sv, "question"); g_object_set_property(G_OBJECT(b), "icon-name", sv)] | [b#set_icon_name "question"] | [Raw.Button.set_icon_name b "question"] |}

Other rules to note:
- When a GObject method or type is a reserved word in OCaml, it is suffixed with an additional underscore e.g. [new] -> [new_], [type] -> [type_]
- If there is a property whose name would conflict with a method when generated, the property accessor is suppressed as OCaml cannot have both, and the method (should) do the same thing.

{2:constants Constants}

Constants are a name for a fixed value in GObject (derived directly from the GIR).

You'll find all constants in each library's [Constants] module (e.g. {!Ocgtk.Constants}), converted to snake_case in OCaml. As they are just OCaml values, you can use them with both the classes layer and the module layer.

{t
   | GObject | C | OCaml |
   | ------- | --- | ----- |
   | BINARY_AGE | [GTK_BINARY_AGE] | {!Ocgtk.Constants.binary_age} |
   | MAX_COMPOSE_LEN | [GTK_MAX_COMPOSE_LEN] | {!Ocgtk.Constants.max_compose_len} |}

{2:enumerations Enumerations}

Enumerations are mapped into each library's [Enums] module (e.g. {!Ocgtk.Enums}).
Each enumeration is a polymorphic variant type.

The enumeration type is mapped to lowercase without spaces e.g. [AccessibleProperty] in GIR becomes [type accessibleproperty] in OCaml.

Each value is kept as {b screaming snake case}:

{t
   | GObject | C | OCaml variant constructor |
   | ------- | --- | ------------------------- |
   | autocomplete | [GTK_ACCESSIBLE_PROPERTY_AUTOCOMPLETE] | [`AUTOCOMPLETE] |
   | multi_selectable | [GTK_ACCESSIBLE_PROPERTY_MULTI_SELECTABLE] | [`MULTI_SELECTABLE] |}

There are also converter functions to convert them to integer form ([<enum>_to_int] e.g. [accessibleproperty_to_int]) and from integer form ([<enum>_of_int] e.g. [accessibleproperty_of_int]). The latter raises [Failure] if you pass an integer that doesn't match any value.

{2:bitfields Bitfields}

Bitfields are also mapped to each library's [Enums] module (e.g. {!Ocgtk.Enums}).
A bitfield is represented in OCaml as a list, where each entry is a value of an associated polymorphic type.

For example, the GObject StyleContextPrintFlags bitfield will be declared as such:

{[

(* StyleContextPrintFlags values type *)
type stylecontextprintflags_flag = 
  [ `NONE | `RECURSE | `SHOW_STYLE | `SHOW_CHANGE ]

(* StyleContextPrintFlags type *)
type stylecontextprintflags = stylecontextprintflags_flag list

]}

Any parameter or return type or property will use the [stylecontextprintflags] type. At runtime, each value is mapped to an integer value and bitwise-ORed together.

{2 Nullable values}

Nullable values in GObject are represented as an OCaml [option] type.

{2:parameters Parameters and return values}

Parameters are passed positionally, in the same order as the C function,
with the object itself first in Layer 1 (and as the receiver in Layer 2):

{[
window#set_default_size 300 200                         (* Layer 2 *)
Raw.Window.set_default_size handle 300 200         (* Layer 1 *)
]}

{b Nullable parameters and return values} use [option], in both
directions:

{[
window#set_title (Some "Hello");    (* parameter that may be NULL *)
window#set_title None;
let label : string option = button#get_label () in
...
]}

{b Out-parameters} are values a C function writes through a pointer
argument instead of returning them. In OCaml they are returned in a tuple
instead: first the C function's own return value (if it has one), then
each out-parameter in the order they appear in C. They don't appear as
arguments at all.

{t | C | OCaml (Layer 1) |
   | --- | --------------- |
   | [void gtk_window_get_default_size (GtkWindow *w, int *width, int *height)] | [Raw.Window.get_default_size : t -> int * int] |
   | [gboolean gtk_label_get_selection_bounds (GtkLabel *l, int *start, int *end)] | [Raw.Label.get_selection_bounds : t -> bool * int * int] |}

When the C function returns a [gboolean] to say whether the out-parameters
were filled in, as [get_selection_bounds] does, check it before using the
other values:

{[
let has_selection, start, end_ =
  Raw.Label.get_selection_bounds label#as_label
in
if has_selection then Printf.printf "selected %d-%d\n" start end_
]}

{b Currently, methods with out-parameters are only available in Layer 1.}
The Layer 2 classes skip them, so call them on the Layer 1 handle as above
(see {{!section-converting}Converting between the layers}).
This is tracked in {{:https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk/issues/201}issue #201}.

Methods that can fail return a [result] instead; see
{{!section-errors}Error values}.

{2:errors Error values}

Any GLib.Error type as a return value or parameter is mapped to {!Ocglib.GError.t} in OCaml.

However, when a method is specified with the [throws] attribute, the OCaml return type will be an OCaml [result] type with {!Ocglib.GError.t} as the [Error] part of the type.

For example, {!Ocgdk.Clipboard.clipboard_t.read_value_finish} declares a return type of [GObject.Value], so its OCaml signature becomes:

{[
class type clipboard_t = object
  ...
  method read_value_finish :
    Ocgio.Async_result.async_result_t ->
    (Gobject.Value.t, GError.t) result
end
]}

(in C, these functions take a [GError**] value to be able to return a newly allocated error object when an error occurs)


{2 Strings}

OCaml strings are mapped transparently in parameters and return types. They are copied in full between GObject strings and OCaml strings in the C layer.

{2:integers Integers}

Plain C [int] and [uint] values ([gint], [guint]) are 32-bit and become ordinary OCaml
[int]. Out-of-range values are not checked.

Similarly, [gssize], [gulong] and [gchar] are converted to [int] and are not checked for out-of-range values. 

A [gint64] becomes an OCaml [int64] and maps with no range issues.

Fixed-width integers get their own types, defined as modules in the
{!Ocglib} library, so that the range is part of the type and a value that
doesn't fit is caught when you convert it:

{t | C type | OCaml type | Range |
   | ------ | ---------- | ----- |
   | [guint8]  | {!Ocglib.UInt8.t}  | 0 to 255 |
   | [gint8]   | {!Ocglib.Int8.t}   | -128 to 127 |
   | [guint16] | {!Ocglib.UInt16.t} | 0 to 65535 |
   | [gint16]  | {!Ocglib.Int16.t}  | -32768 to 32767 |
   | [guint32] | {!Ocglib.UInt32.t} | 0 to 4294967295 |
   | [gint32]  | {!Ocglib.Int32.t}  | -2147483648 to 2147483647 |
   | [guint64] | {!Ocglib.UInt64.t} | 0 to 2{^64}-1 |
   | [gsize]   | {!Ocglib.Gsize.t}  | 0 to [max_int] |}

Apart from [UInt64], these are all [private int], so they cost nothing at
runtime. You can read them as an [int] with [to_int], but you can only create
one through [of_int]. [UInt64.t] is abstract and holds an [int64] internally.

Each module has the same small API:

- [of_int : int -> t] raises [Invalid_argument] if the value is out of range.
- [to_int : t -> int] converts back.
- [min_int], [max_int] and [zero] (or [minus_one] for signed types) are constants of
  type [t]. [min_value] and [max_value] give the same limits as plain [int]s.

[UInt64] and [Gsize] differ slightly. [UInt64] has no [min_int] or
[min_value], but adds [one], [compare] and [equal] (use these rather than
polymorphic comparison, since it is stored as an [int64]). [Gsize] has [zero]
and [max_int] but no [min_int].

{[
let n = UInt16.of_int 300 in
let width : int = UInt16.to_int n + 1 in
ignore (UInt8.of_int 256) (* raises Invalid_argument *)
]}

Like the rest of {!Ocglib}, they are reached as [Ocglib.UInt8],
[Ocglib.Int32], and so on. If you [open Ocglib], [Int32] shadows
[Stdlib.Int32], so write [Stdlib.Int32] if you need OCaml's boxed
[int32]. The fixed-width modules (all except [Gsize]) are also grouped
under {!Ocglib.Bounded_int}, for example [Ocglib.Bounded_int.UInt16].

{2 Lists and arrays}

GLib has its own list and array types which still appear in some APIs (particularly older ones, such as in Gio).

[GList] (doubly-linked) and [GSList] (singly-linked) are both mapped to OCaml [list] type. At runtime, these are converted to and from by walking the list and constructing a new one.

Arrays are converted to the OCaml [array] type, and are similarly converted by creating a new array when moving back and forth between C and OCaml.

In both cases, elements are converted as well. For primitive types like integers and strings, this is a full by-copy conversion, while reference types like objects are passed over without a full copy. (The records implementation currently depends on whether they are boxed, and may be buggy - see the next section).

{2:records Record types}

Record types are currently stored and exposed as OCaml objects in Layer 2. There is pending work to expose their fields correctly in OCaml, which may change this interface.

{1 Version availability}

The bindings are generated from GIR files, which have been vendored
in the repository for convenience (last update was from GNOME 49 / GTK 4.20).

The code that is generated from them with [gir_gen] will build and run
against any GTK 4 release: you don't need GTK 4.20 to use them. 

Keep in mind however:

- APIs in newer GTK versions will not be available
- we have only verified that older versions will compile in a range of
  currently supported Linux distributions (as of October 2026). We may
  not have the correct overrides in place to compile on much older
  distributions (see version overrides below)

{2 Version Overrides - how newer APIs are guarded at compile time}

Each C stub for a function, method or class/interface added in releases
subsequent to GTK 4.0 is wrapped in a version check. 

For example, [gtk_window_set_gravity] was added in GTK 4.20:

{@c[
#if GTK_CHECK_VERSION(4,20,0)
CAMLprim value ml_gtk_window_set_gravity(value self, value arg1)
{
  /* calls gtk_window_set_gravity */
}
#else
CAMLprim value ml_gtk_window_set_gravity(value self, value arg1)
{
  caml_failwith("Window requires GTK >= 4.20");
}
#endif
]}

In the OCaml code, every API is present (in both Layer 1 and Layer 2)
so your code will always compile. However, if your code is compiled and
run against an older GTK version, calling a newer API will result in a
[Failure] exception at runtime.

Other libraries such as ocglib and ocgio use the same guards from their
respective C dependencies.

The next section covers how to manage this better at runtime if you are
using newer APIs and want to make sure your code behaves correctly at
runtime when used on older systems.

{2 Checking the newer version in your code}

If your application should work on older GTK releases, check the version
before calling a newer API instead of catching [Failure]. The {!Ocgtk.Version}
module gives you both versions:

{[
open Ocgtk

let place_window window =
  if Version.runtime_at_least 4 20 0 then
    window#set_gravity `TOP_LEFT
]}

- {!Ocgtk.Version.runtime} and {!Ocgtk.Version.runtime_at_least} give the version
  of the GTK library that is actually loaded.
- {!Ocgtk.Version.compile_time} gives the version of the headers ocgtk was
  built against. Stubs above this version always raise [Failure], even if
  the runtime library is newer.

To be safe, check both: an API works only if the compile-time version and
the runtime version are each at least the version that added it.

{b NOTE:} [Version] is currently only available in ocgtk - other libraries
don't currently have a similar module.

{2 How versioning works}

GIR provides metadata that indicates when new methods or classes were
introduced, and this is used directly by gir_gen when generating code.

However, this metadata is not always added, and is not present for
some constructs (e.g. constants). For those, we maintain version overrides
(see the {{:https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk/blob/main/architecture/gir_gen/overrides.md}overrides design}).

{%html: <script type="module">
import mermaid from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mermaid@12.1.0/dist/mermaid.esm.min.mjs';
mermaid.initialize({ startOnLoad: false, theme: 'neutral' });
// Render each {@mermaid[...]} block with its own id. mermaid.run() derives
// ids from Date.now(), so diagrams rendered in the same millisecond collide.
let n = 0;
for (const code of document.querySelectorAll('pre.language-mermaid > code')) {
  const { svg } = await mermaid.render(`ocgtk-diagram-${n++}`, code.textContent);
  const div = document.createElement('div');
  div.className = 'ocgtk-diagram';
  div.innerHTML = svg;
  code.parentElement.replaceWith(div);
}
</script> %}
